      "transcript": "NARRATOR: Part 1. You will hear a man arranging temporary storage for household belongings. First, look at Questions 1 to 6.\nNARRATOR: Now listen carefully and answer Questions 1 to 6.\nAMELIA REED: Good morning. You’ve reached Merton Self Store. My name’s Amelia. How can I help?\nNOAH CARMICHAEL: Hello. I’m moving out next month, but my new flat won’t be ready for about six weeks. I need somewhere secure during that gap.\nAMELIA: That should be straightforward. I’ll take a few details and work out what size of unit you need. Could I start with your full name?\nNOAH: It’s Noah Carmichael.\nAMELIA: Thank you. And when would you like the storage period to begin?\nNOAH: I first wrote the sixteenth of October because my brother offered his van that day. He has since learned he won’t be free until two days later, so the storage should actually start on the eighteenth.\nAMELIA: The eighteenth of October. Fine. Now, roughly how much are you storing? A one-bedroom flat can mean very different things.\nNOAH: There’s no sofa or dining table. The large pieces are a double mattress, a narrow bookcase, six dining chairs and a bicycle. Then I’ve got about twenty boxes. I looked online and wondered whether a forty-square-foot unit would be safest.\nAMELIA: Forty would leave quite a lot of unused space. Twenty-five would be too tight once the mattress is inside, but the thirty-five-square-foot unit should take everything and still leave a passage down the middle. I’ll recommend thirty-five.\nNOAH: That sounds better. I’d rather not pay for empty floor space.\nAMELIA: Exactly. Our ordinary access hours finish at seven in the evening. Would that suit you?\nNOAH: Not every day. I can usually arrive by eight, but on the first evening I’ll be travelling with the van and may not get there until nine. Could I have access until half past nine?\nAMELIA: Yes. We can put late access on your account, which will let your entry code work until nine thirty p.m. After that, the security system closes the building automatically.\nNOAH: Nine thirty is plenty.\nAMELIA: Are there any unusually fragile belongings? I don’t need a list of ordinary kitchen glass, but we record larger breakable items for insurance.\nNOAH: I’ve got three framed prints, though those have plastic rather than glass. The things I’m concerned about are two framed mirrors. They belonged to my grandparents, so I’ll wrap them separately.\nAMELIA: I’ll note two framed mirrors. And finally, what would it cost to replace everything you plan to store?\nNOAH: I made a list yesterday. The furniture, boxes and mirrors came to four thousand pounds. I’d forgotten the bicycle, though, and replacing that would cost about eight hundred. So the total should be four thousand eight hundred.\nAMELIA: Good. I’ve entered a declared value of four thousand eight hundred pounds.\nNARRATOR: Now look at Questions 7 to 10.\nNARRATOR: Now listen and answer Questions 7 to 10.\nAMELIA: Let’s go through the optional services. Do you already have transport for the first day?\nNOAH: Possibly. My brother offered his van, but it’s smaller than I expected and we’d probably need three journeys.\nAMELIA: Our collection van is large enough for your inventory and comes with a driver and loading trolley. A weekend collection would be fifty-six pounds. As you’re coming on a Thursday, the price is forty-eight.\nNOAH: Forty-eight is reasonable. Please add that.\nAMELIA: Would you also like the protective packing set? It contains four reusable blankets and eight corner guards. The straps belong to the van, so the driver takes those away afterwards, but you keep the guards for the whole rental.\nNOAH: Yes, the corner guards will be useful for the mirrors.\nAMELIA: The set costs twelve pounds. You’ll also need a lock. We used to supply one with a key, but customers often misplaced the key, so our standard lock is now a combination model. You choose the four-digit code when you arrive.\nNOAH: A combination lock is better for me. I won’t have another key to carry.\nAMELIA: There is a refundable deposit as well. Once the unit has been emptied and checked, we normally return it within five working days. Some banks take another day or two to display the payment, but the refund leaves us within five.\nNOAH: Fine. Can I pay everything by card now?\nAMELIA: Yes. I’ll send a secure payment link with the booking summary. Check the dates and declared value before paying, and call us if anything changes.\nNOAH: I will. Thanks for making that simple.\nNARRATOR: That is the end of Part 1. Check your answers before Part 2 begins.",
