      "transcript": "NARRATOR: Part 2. You will hear a fire-safety officer giving advice to residents and explaining a free home-inspection service. First, look at Questions 11 to 15.\nNARRATOR: Now listen carefully and answer Questions 11 to 15.\nKAREN MERCER: Good evening. I’m Karen Mercer from Northgate Fire and Rescue. I’ll begin with five situations that residents often ask about. There is no single device that solves every problem, so listen carefully to which action fits each situation.\nCALLUM: The first concerns cooking. If steam or cooking fumes regularly set off the smoke alarm beside your kitchen, don’t remove its battery. Fit a heat alarm there, while keeping an ordinary smoke alarm in the nearby hall. A fire blanket can help with a small pan fire, but it will not solve repeated false alarms.\nCALLUM: Second, some residents have alarms but remain uncertain about the rest of the property. They may not know whether an elderly relative could hear an alarm from a back bedroom. Rather than buying equipment at random, request a free inspection. An officer will assess the whole home, test existing devices and recommend what is actually needed.\nCALLUM: The third situation is electrical. A television, portable heater and kettle should never share a lightweight extension lead. Switching one appliance off most of the time is not enough. Replace the extension lead with one rated for the required load, or have additional wall sockets installed by a qualified electrician. Moving the heater does not remove the overloaded connection.\nCALLUM: Fourth, many serious fires spread after people have gone to bed. Closing internal doors before sleeping slows smoke and heat and gives the alarm more time to wake everyone. Do not lock those doors, because that could delay an escape; simply shut them.\nCALLUM: The final situation involves a portable heater beside an armchair. Move the heater at least a metre from upholstered furniture, curtains and drying clothes. A fire blanket belongs in the kitchen and is not a substitute for safe distance. Also place the heater where it cannot be knocked over.\nCALLUM: These recommendations deal with the immediate risk in each example. If two or more apply to your home, follow each relevant action rather than choosing only one.\nNARRATOR: Now look at Questions 16 to 20.\nNARRATOR: Now listen and answer Questions 16 to 20.\nCALLUM: I’ll now explain our free home-inspection visits. This month, the team is concentrating on the north side of Mereford. Monday’s appointments are in Quarry Lane. On Tuesday, officers will visit Larkhill Road, and Wednesday has been set aside for Northgate Crescent. If your street is not listed, you can still request a later appointment through the council website.\nCALLUM: Please remember that genuine inspectors never arrive without an appointment. Every officer will carry photo ID showing the Northgate service badge and an identification number. A uniform by itself is not proof. Ask to see the photo ID before opening the door, and telephone the service if the face or number does not match the appointment message.\nCALLUM: During the visit, we will check alarm coverage. A single-storey home usually needs a smoke alarm in the hall between the living area and bedrooms. In a two-storey house, there should be one downstairs as well, but the second alarm must be on the upstairs landing, where it can be heard from every bedroom. An alarm inside only the main bedroom does not provide adequate coverage.\nCALLUM: Testing is just as important as location. Some packaging suggests a monthly check, and our reminder messages were once sent on the first day of each month. Current guidance is to press the test button every week. Choose a regular day so it becomes a habit. If an alarm chirps because the battery is low, replace the battery immediately rather than waiting for the weekly test.\nCALLUM: Appointments last about forty minutes. If you need to change yours, do not call the emergency station. Use the inspection line on zero one six three two, nine six zero, four one two. I’ll repeat that: zero one six three two, nine six zero, four one two. The line is staffed from nine until five. Outside those hours, leave your name and appointment date, but never leave medical or banking information.\nCALLUM: The inspection is free. Officers may supply standard alarms where none are fitted, but they do not sell products, collect donations or ask to see financial documents. If electrical work is needed, you will receive a written recommendation.\nCALLUM: Thank you. Booking cards are available by the exit.\nNARRATOR: That is the end of Part 2. Check your answers before Part 3 begins.",
