      "instructions": "21-30: Follow the printed instruction and word limit.",
      "transcript": "NARRATOR: Part 3. You will hear two students discussing plans for an oral-history archive with their tutor. First, look at Questions 21 to 25.\nNARRATOR: Now listen carefully and answer Questions 21 to 25.\nAISHA RAHMAN: Naomi, Isaac, I’ve read your proposal for an archive about people who work through the night. The central idea is strong, but the sample needs reconsidering before you recruit anyone.\nNAOMI BELL: Is twelve interviewees too ambitious? We could reduce it to eight. I also wondered whether the age range was narrow, although our youngest participant is twenty-six and the oldest is sixty-one.\nAISHA: Neither is the main issue. All twelve people on your list work in health care: nurses, laboratory technicians and ambulance dispatchers. That would tell us a great deal about one sector, but not about night work more generally. You need people from different occupations, even if the final sample is slightly smaller.\nISAAC WEBB: We could approach a baker, a freight controller and someone from a hotel, then keep some of the hospital staff. That would give us contrasting reasons for being awake at those hours.\nAISHA: Exactly. Before the main interviews, though, I want you to run a pilot. The council offered two employees who currently work nights, but their manager would have to attend, which could limit what they say. The archive’s experienced interviewers would give useful technical advice, yet they are too familiar with oral-history conventions to behave like ordinary participants.\nNAOMI: What about the archive volunteers who used to do night shifts? Three of them said they’d be happy to help.\nAISHA: Use those volunteers for the pilot. They understand why the material will be preserved, but they have not conducted interviews themselves. Their feedback should expose unclear questions without using any of your main participants.\nISAAC: We also need to settle the recording format. Video would capture facial expression, and initially we thought of making both video and audio versions.\nNAOMI: But two potential participants said a camera would make them self-conscious. Video files would also require far more storage. If we record audio only, we can still make careful notes about gestures that clarify what a speaker means.\nAISHA: I agree. Choose audio only. Consistent, well-recorded sound is more valuable here than poor video, and people may speak more freely without a lens pointed at them.\nISAAC: For the interview itself, I drafted a chronological sequence: childhood, first job, first night shift and then the present.\nNAOMI: That order is tidy, but it may force people to spend half the session on background. A fixed list of identical questions would make comparison easy, although it could sound like a survey rather than an oral history.\nAISHA: Prepare topic cards instead: sleep, food, family contact, quiet periods, emergencies and the journey home. After a short opening, let each participant choose which card to discuss next. You’ll cover the same broad areas, but the order will reflect what matters to that person.\nISAAC: That should also make the interview feel less controlled. We can keep the chronological questions only as prompts if somebody struggles to begin.\nNAOMI: How should we judge whether the archive succeeds? Our proposal says twenty completed interviews, and we estimated that each would last at least forty-five minutes.\nAISHA: Numbers and length are production targets, not the main measure of value. A long set of similar accounts would add little. Your primary measure should be the range of occupations represented. Within that range, you can report differences in age and experience.\nNAOMI: Right. We’ll revise the recruitment table so occupational range is visible before we invite anyone else.\nNARRATOR: Now look at Questions 26 to 30.\nNARRATOR: Now listen and answer Questions 26 to 30.\nAISHA: Let’s consider the practical problems you identified. First, privacy. Several participants are willing to describe mistakes or difficult incidents, but they do not want neighbours or employers to find their names online.\nISAAC: We planned a pre-interview phone call to explain how the website works. Would that be enough if they signed a consent form afterwards?\nAISHA: The call is good practice, but it does not protect identity. Offer pseudonyms for the public catalogue and recording. Keep the real-name list in an encrypted file available only to the archive manager. Participants can still choose to be named.\nNAOMI: The next problem is fatigue. Someone finishing at six in the morning may not manage a ninety-minute interview, even after a break.\nISAAC: We could cut every interview to forty minutes, but then an important story might be stopped just as it becomes detailed.\nAISHA: Divide it into two shorter sessions. Agree the second date before ending the first, and begin the follow-up with a brief summary. That gives people time to rest and often allows them to remember useful details between sessions.\nNAOMI: That leads to another issue. Memories may surface during the night, when neither of us is available to record an interview. One participant said his clearest recollections come during the quiet hour before dawn.\nISAAC: Could we ask people to use a phone app? It might record notifications and other sounds as well, and not everyone owns the same kind of phone.\nAISHA: Lend each participant a simple recorder with a single large button. They can capture a short voice note when a memory occurs, then discuss it fully at the next session. You should copy the file and erase the device before it goes to somebody else.\nISAAC: Researchers will eventually need to find relevant passages. A hundred hours of sound would be impossible to search by starting at the beginning of every recording.\nNAOMI: Removing pauses and background sound might shorten it, but we’d lose the atmosphere of the interviews.\nAISHA: Retain the original recordings and create a keyword index. For each file, record the time at which subjects such as transport, meals or isolation begin. A researcher can then jump to the relevant passage without altering the source audio.\nNAOMI: Finally, the automated transcripts misheard several names and changed technical terms. Hiring a professional would reduce those errors, but it would use most of our budget.\nISAAC: We could correct the obvious mistakes ourselves and mark any word we cannot understand.\nAISHA: Do that as a first pass, then ask each participant to approve the transcript. They can correct specialised vocabulary and point out where the written wording changes their intended meaning. Keep a record of every amendment rather than silently replacing the original.\nNAOMI: So we use pseudonyms, split long interviews, lend recorders, build a keyword index and return transcripts for approval. That gives us a clear revision list.\nAISHA: Good. Send me the revised protocol after the pilot, and we’ll check it before recruitment begins.\nNARRATOR: That is the end of Part 3. Check your answers before Part 4 begins.",
