      "title": "Listening — Part 4: Set 21 Part 4",
      "instructions": "31-40: Follow the printed instruction and word limit.",
      "transcript": "NARRATOR: Part 4. You will hear a lecture about the recovery and interpretation of ancient seeds from waterlogged archaeological sites. First, look at Questions 31 to 36.\nNARRATOR: Now listen carefully and answer Questions 31 to 36.\nDR ZOE MADDEN: Today we’ll examine a source of archaeological evidence that is physically small but remarkably informative: waterlogged seeds. Plant remains normally disappear quickly. In dry soil, insects, fungi and other organisms break down soft tissue until little survives. Waterlogging changes that process because the spaces in the soil remain filled with water. Oxygen cannot circulate, and the microorganisms responsible for most decay become far less active. The seed is not turned to stone; it remains organic and fragile, but decomposition slows dramatically.\nZOE: Preservation is rarely uniform across a site. An open surface may dry repeatedly and destroy its plant material, while a nearby pit stays saturated. Wooden structures are especially useful. A lining of planks around a well, or the base of a timber drain, can form a pocket that retains water even when the surrounding soil becomes drier. Archaeologists therefore record the precise deposit around every sample rather than describing an entire excavation as simply waterlogged.\nZOE: Collection begins before the seeds themselves are visible. Excavators place measured blocks of dark sediment in sealed buckets. Cloth bags are appropriate for dry pottery, but they allow moisture to escape, so they are unsuitable here. Each bucket receives the context number, depth and collection date on two labels: one outside and one protected beneath the lid. Until processing, the buckets are kept cool, although freezing is avoided because it can damage delicate remains.\nZOE: In the laboratory, water is used to loosen the sediment over a sequence of sieves. The upper sieve has the widest openings. It catches gravel, along with pieces of wood or bone that must be examined separately. The material passing through then reaches progressively finer mesh. This staged approach is slower than forcing everything through one very fine screen, but it prevents the mesh from becoming blocked and reduces damage to the smallest seeds.\nZOE: After washing, the residue is sorted while still damp. Large cereal grains may be recognised by eye, but many wild plants produce seeds only a millimetre across. Final identification is therefore carried out under a microscope. The analyst looks at overall shape, surface texture and the scar where the seed was attached to the parent plant. Colour alone is unreliable because centuries in wet sediment can darken unrelated species in similar ways.\nZOE: Identification is comparative rather than intuitive. Archaeological specimens are placed beside modern seeds of known species held in a reference collection. Good collections include natural variation: a seed from a dry hillside may be narrower than one of the same species grown beside a stream. Published photographs are helpful, but they flatten texture and scale, so they cannot fully replace physical reference material.\nZOE: Analysts must also decide whether a seed is genuinely ancient. Modern roots can penetrate an old deposit and carry recent seeds down through cracks. Earth-moving animals may do the same. Material that entered in this way is recorded as contamination, not quietly removed from the data. A pale colour, intact soft tissue or a concentration around a living root can indicate recent origin, although several signs should be considered together.\nZOE: Once species and context have been established, the seeds can address larger questions: what people ate, which plants grew nearby and how crops were processed. However, a list of species is only the beginning. Interpretation depends on where different remains occur and on which stages of human activity could have produced that pattern.\nNARRATOR: Now look at Questions 37 to 40.\nNARRATOR: Now listen and answer Questions 37 to 40.\nZOE: Consider a case study from a small wetland settlement. Researchers took equal-volume samples from an outer yard, a roofed processing room and the area around a central hearth. Because every sample represented the same quantity of sediment, the relative abundance of plant remains could be compared. The team separated cereal grain from chaff, weed seeds and fragments of stalk, then examined how these categories were distributed.\nZOE: The outer yard contained very little clean grain but large numbers of broken stalks. Those fragments are produced when harvested cereal heads are beaten to separate grain from the main stems. In other words, their concentration indicates threshing. The activity was probably kept outside because it required space and generated a great deal of dust. A few grains in the yard do not contradict that conclusion; some loss is inevitable during beating.\nZOE: The covered room presented a different pattern. Coarse stems were scarce, while small weed seeds occurred in dense patches beside low working platforms. Large weeds can be removed earlier by hand, but seeds similar in size to cereal grain survive until a finer screen is used. The evidence therefore points to fine sieving inside the covered room. Shelter would have mattered at this stage because a sudden shower could wet cleaned grain and make it difficult to store.\nZOE: Near the hearth, most grain was intact, but its surface was darkened and slightly cracked by heat. One explanation would be an accidental building fire. However, the same limited heating appeared in several occupation layers, while roof timbers showed no burning. Researchers concluded that the grain had probably been roasted deliberately in small batches. Light roasting can loosen the husk of some cereal varieties and also changes flavour.\nZOE: Finally, the team looked for evidence of long-term storage. A large granary often supports beetles and other insects that feed specifically on stored cereal. Their bodies survive well in the same waterlogged deposits as seeds. Here, however, there were no storage pests. This absence cannot prove that no grain was ever kept on the site, but combined with the small processing batches, it suggests that supplies moved through fairly quickly rather than remaining for an entire season.\nZOE: Taken together, the distributions form a sequence. Initial threshing occurred in the open yard. Fine cleaning took place under cover. Selected batches were roasted beside the hearth and then used or moved elsewhere before pests became established. No single seed reveals that organisation. It emerges only because recovery methods were consistent and every category, including apparent waste, was linked to its exact context.\nZOE: This is the wider value of archaeobotany. Tiny remains can reconstruct decisions about labour, space and timing, provided that excavation, laboratory processing and interpretation are treated as parts of one evidential chain.\nNARRATOR: That is the end of Part 4, and the end of the Listening practice test. You now have a short time to check your answers.",
