      "passage": "A A dry-stone wall may look informal, yet its construction follows a disciplined sequence rather than the chance piling of rock. Its recognised profile and finish respond to the size, shape and composition of local stone, so a form familiar in one district need not be copied elsewhere. Before building starts, usable material is sorted into broad working groups. Building stones will form the two visible faces, through stones are reserved to connect those faces, and coping stones are kept for the top.\n\nB The first constructed layer is the footing, which gives remaining courses a firm base. Work begins by clearing weak material until firm ground or firm stones are reached and, where the guidance calls for it, preparing a shallow trench. The largest suitable stones are normally chosen for this beginning and are arranged so that they sit securely. Irregular spaces beneath them are packed with stone rather than left to make the footing rock. Fine loose fill is not a substitute for careful placement, and the bounded guidance also excludes concrete and mortar from this dry construction.\n\nC From the footing upward, the two faces do not rise vertically at a constant width. They follow a batter: an even inward taper that makes the wall wider at its base and narrower at its top. Simple guide lines or frames can help a builder judge this changing profile while the courses rise. The important quality is consistency along the work, not a sudden change at one convenient point. A local profile may be slight, but it should remain even on both faces. Visible bulges or depressions are warnings that the intended line has been lost and that the wall may be weakened.\n\nD The wall is built as two faces with a centre between them, but the faces must not behave as unrelated skins. Face stones are graded as the work rises, and their longer dimension is directed inward rather than laid along the visible surface. Vertical joints are covered by stones in the course above, avoiding continuous gaps through successive layers. Neighbouring pieces are brought into useful contact and the face is checked for openings that reveal daylight. At selected stages, through stones extend across the wall and tie its two sides together. The result depends on overlap, inward length and face connections working together throughout the build.\n\nE As each face course is formed, the middle is filled with hearting, the smaller irregular stone used between the faces. Hearting is placed and packed during the same course, rather than tipped into a deep hollow after the faces have climbed far ahead. Pieces are selected to occupy awkward spaces, while very small loose material is not treated as a complete solution. The centre should become compact without being mistaken for a sealed block. Packing supports the orderly relationship between the two faces and prepares an even level for the next course. A qualitative check is direct: the finished work and its hearting should feel solid and should not move when tested by impact.\n\nF The final full course does not by itself complete the wall. Coping stones are fitted along the top to make an even upper line and are packed tightly enough to remain steady. Their weight is directed onto the top face stones rather than onto an uncertain empty space. At a wall head or exposed end, runners or tying pieces and carefully placed end coping provide a stable finish. The builder checks the height visually along the work and adjusts individual stones before declaring the line complete. The close of the sequence therefore repeats the same habits seen below: selection, contact, packing and checking.\n\nG Repair begins with a decision about where reliable construction resumes, not with covering the most obvious surface gap. Damaged work is dismantled back to sound construction so that the new section can join material that is still firm. Recovered stones are sorted again, preserving suitable building, through and coping pieces for their proper positions. Rebuilding then follows the original order: establish the base, restore an even batter, connect the faces, pack the hearting and finish the top. An even top line and consistent face profile allow the rebuilt section to meet the retained wall without an abrupt change. What appears to be removal is therefore constructive preparation: sound work supplies the starting point for sound rebuilding.",
