      "passage": "A When hydrogeologists assign a date using a dissolved marker, the result needs careful wording. A tracer age refers to when a particular chemical substance entered the water; it is not automatically the age of the water itself. That distinction matters because each tracer carries a record shaped by its own history and properties. The interpretation is conditional rather than a simple reading from a chemical dial. These applications do not turn a measured concentration into a direct clock for one indivisible body of water. Instead, the date is an inference whose meaning depends on the chosen tracer and the assumptions used to interpret it.\n\nB The broad label environmental tracer includes CFCs, tritium and other chemical or isotopic substances measured in groundwater. Their historical records differ, which is one reason they are considered together rather than treated as interchangeable. The atmospheric tritium signal discussed in the evidence is linked to nuclear detonations during the 1950s and early 1960s. CFCs, by contrast, are associated with refrigeration and other uses extending from the 1950s through the 1980s. Those source histories supply chronological reference points, but they do not by themselves explain every process between atmosphere and sample. Nor does the shared label mean that the substances have identical origins or respond identically in an analysis.\n\nC Each candidate tracer offers a bounded piece of chronological evidence rather than a complete timetable. For CFCs, roughly fifty years of recorded atmospheric amounts provide one basis for groundwater dating. Tritium has a half-life of 12.4 years and is useful in work on young groundwater, although tritium data alone can be difficult to evaluate for age. Another substance, sulfur hexafluoride or SF6, entered industrial production in 1953 in connection with gas-filled high-voltage electrical switches. The dates and numerical property in these records are informative, yet none makes a single tracer universally sufficient. The responsible question is not which marker always wins, but what temporal constraint each record can defensibly provide.\n\nD Measured values may also require treatment before they are used to calibrate an age distribution. In one cited investigation, tracer concentrations other than tritium received analytical corrections before calibration. Relevant uncertainty factors listed in the evidence include the water-table temperature during recharge and the entrapment of excess air at recharge. These factors are named here because they affect interpretation, not to supply a general account of their chemistry. The passage likewise does not assume that one factor is always larger than the other. A correction is part of the route from an observed concentration to an inference; it is not evidence that the uncorrected number directly states an age.\n\nE A further complication arises when a well contains a mixture of younger and older water. Such mixing can affect the CFC concentration measured in the well. Consequently, assigning one literal recharge date to the whole sample can conceal the presence of different components. An apparent tracer age in this setting is not a birth certificate for a single parcel. Researchers instead use the concentration as evidence within a model that can represent an age distribution. The model may still be useful, but its output remains a model-mediated estimate. This is why concentration, correction, mixing and age cannot be treated as four names for the same observation.\n\nF One approach is to fit a lumped-parameter model to several environmental-tracer observations. In this usage, the model is a compact way of estimating a distribution of recharge times rather than identifying the exact history of every molecule. The fitting process draws on corrected analytical values and on assumptions about how ages are distributed. Its sensitivity can be tested by changing parameters instead of accepting the first output as inevitable. For example, changing a fitted dispersion parameter can produce a younger mean age. That shift does not show that the water itself has suddenly become younger; it shows that the inferred mean depends on model form and parameter choice. Agreement across tracers is therefore evidence to examine, not permission to erase the assumptions behind it.\n\nG The practical value of age information lies in disciplined use rather than claims of perfect dating. An estimated age distribution can contribute to recharge-rate calculations and can improve the structure or calibration of a hydrologic model. Those uses connect observations in wells with questions about movement through a water-supply catchment. They are strongest when analysts state which tracer record, correction and mixing assumptions support the estimate. A result should therefore be read as conditional evidence, open to revision if another tracer or model reveals a different distribution. The central lesson is methodological: environmental tracers constrain recharge history, but only through a chain of interpretation. Protecting every link in that chain makes the conclusion narrower, and also more useful.",
