      "passage": "A For much of its modern history, the kilogram was defined through the mass of the International Prototype of the Kilogram. That arrangement made a single artefact conceptually central to the unit used elsewhere. Replacing the artefact basis promised a definition not tied to one stored object. A definition states what the unit is; laboratories still need a defensible way to establish mass values from it. The argument of this passage is that universality at the definitional level is real, while universality in practical access must be achieved through methods, comparison and dissemination.\n\nB The kilogram is now defined using a fixed value of the Planck constant, and the former prototype no longer realises mass. The selected numerical value of that constant preserved continuity with the previous definition of the unit. To realise a unit is to establish a value, together with its associated uncertainty, for a quantity of the same kind as that unit. The uncertainty belongs inside the statement of a realisation, not outside it as an embarrassing afterthought. Thus the new definition removes dependence on one defining artefact without removing the need for measurement work or physical mass standards used in calibration.\n\nC One primary route employs a Kibble balance. At the level needed here, its defining relation is a comparison in which electrical power is equated with mechanical power. That concise description is important because it explains the kind of bridge the instrument creates without turning the passage into an operating manual. The route connects a mass realisation to experimentally established quantities, but the connection must still be made by an actual instrument. It would therefore be a mistake to argue that fixing a constant has made realisation equipment unnecessary.\n\nD The x-ray crystal density method, or XRCD, expresses the mass of a pure substance in terms of its number of elementary entities. In this approach an unknown mass can be compared with the mass of a specified-isotope atom by counting atoms in a crystal. Single crystals of silicon are often used because large crystals with high chemical purity and no dislocations can be obtained. Together, the Kibble and XRCD routes show that the definition does not depend on only one practical technique. Two independent methods can reach relative uncertainties within a few parts in one hundred million, but that capability does not guarantee that every result will agree automatically. Method diversity strengthens the system only when the outcomes can also be compared.\n\nE International comparison is therefore part of the achievement, not a ceremonial step added after it. The BIPM, coordinating with the Consultative Committee for Mass and Related Quantities, organises a key comparison for laboratories operating primary realisation methods. When individual results remain dispersed, national metrology institutes with a kilogram realisation use a consensus value to preserve international equivalence. The value is internationally agreed, but agreement does not replace measurement. This arrangement qualifies the simple claim that independent access to a constant must immediately produce identical masses everywhere. A universal definition supports comparison; comparison and consensus make practical equivalence more robust.\n\nF Mass dissemination passes the kilogram onward through calibrations of customer weights and weight sets. The definition itself does not imply or favour any particular method of realisation, so this calibration-based route remains necessary rather than obsolete. A newer tabletop Kibble route is described as enabling gram-level realisation on site, linked to voltage and resistance standards and ultimately to the Planck constant. This is a specific traceability route, not a claim that every institution already has equal capability. The chain from definition to primary method, comparison, agreed value and calibration shows why changing the first link cannot replace all the later ones. Later steps preserve a usable relation between primary work and ordinary weights.\n\nG A one-kilogram Kibble balance at a national metrology institute can occupy a room, cost extremely large sums and require expert operators. Tabletop work points toward on-site realisation at the gram level and may bring the practical route closer to more laboratories. But scaling farther from the one-kilogram benchmark adds uncertainties, so smaller equipment is not simply a complete replacement with the size reduced. The contrast reveals a premise that optimistic accounts often omit: a method can be permitted by a universal definition and still demand scarce equipment or knowledge. Progress in instrument design may narrow that gap, but the definition alone does not tell us when, where or for whom it will disappear.\n\nH The strongest counterclaim is that once any competent laboratory may realise the kilogram from a constant, decentralisation has already been accomplished. That claim correctly recognises the freedom created by a method-neutral definition, but it compresses permission, capability and equivalence into one idea. A laboratory needs a workable method; its result must sit within comparison and consensus arrangements; and users receive the unit through calibration. Room-scale instruments and emerging tabletop routes demonstrate that practical capability can vary even under the same definition. The removal of a unique defining artefact has solved one specific dependency, while reliable universality in use still ultimately depends on realisation, comparison and dissemination. Equal definition is therefore a foundation for access, not evidence that equal access has already arrived.",
